            <summary class="font-semibold text-white cursor-pointer list-none flex justify-between items-center text-sm">
              <span>Why is argon at 87 K preferred over N₂ for micropore analysis?</span>
              <span class="text-emerald-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
              Nitrogen has a permanent quadrupole moment that interacts specifically with exposed metal ions (open coordination sites) in MOFs, causing orientational shifts and uncertainties in the effective cross-sectional area (σ). Monatomic argon (87.27 K) eliminates quadrupole interactions, providing more accurate pore-size distributions.
            </p>
